{"summary": "`merge()` now does what its docstring says. Intervals that share an endpoint (`[1,3]` and `[3,4]`) or touch (`[1,2]` and `[3,4]`) now become one interval. Before, both stayed separate.

The bug was the merge check in `intervals.py:7`. It said `s < out[-1][1]`, which only caught overlaps where one interval starts strictly inside the other. It's now `s <= out[-1][1] + 1`, which also counts a shared endpoint or the next integer as touching.

I added five tests to `test_intervals.py`: shared endpoint, touching, a real gap that stays split, unsorted input with duplicates, and an empty list. `python -m unittest -v` ran 6 tests and all passed."}
